{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1555039-pentex-in-v-tm","id":1555039,"title":"Pentex in V:TM","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-11-03T18:04:54-08:00","id":1555039,"title":"Pentex in V:TM","content":"I'm debating doing another article that would involve Pentex in Vampire: The Masquerade. This is probably not a good time to do the company since I'm not sure the conglomerate still exists in 5th Edition but I'm not, NOT sure (if that makes any sense). Pentex, Endron, and all the other old guys still exist in EARTHBLOOD but the sidebar of 5th Edition makes it clear that they want to hue much closer to real life inspirations than the more stylized sarcastic parodies of previous games.<br /> <br /> In any case, this is a thread about the possible uses for the corporation that is Vought Industries meets Wolfram and Hart in a vampire game.<br /> <br /> How would you use them in V:TM?<br /> <br /> Mostly as now I just use them as background since it makes the world feel better if there's Endron, O'Tolleys, and other franchises sprinkled into the backgrounds but I think there's a lot of potential for the undead to get involved in the kind of shadowy finance games that might otherwise be out of the scope of your typical undead's adventures.<br /> <br /> It also could well be a place to use it and leave vampires ignorant in-character but the players know what's going on. What are these hideous mutated things? Creatures of the Sabbat? Tremere mutations? Who is secretly in control of this grand conspiracy the Elders in the city can't deal with? What is this obsession with this random park in the middle of nowhere?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 11-03-2025, 09:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-03T18:50:02-08:00","id":1555044,"title":"","content":"I think you should do it. And the fact it may not exist is actually even more reason to do it.<br /> <br /> But I do think you should lean more on real life inspirations instead of dark comedy. Vampire can be cheese, but it doesn't deal well with direct comedy in the level of standard PENTEX.<br /> <br /> Otherwise I think you have a solid idea and it will improve the game regardless of what they officially do later in the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-03T19:35:03-08:00","id":1555046,"title":"","content":"What are you using Pentex for?<br /> <br /> Cheeky continuity nods? Not the best use, but I suppose you could. At that point, it's only a mention in the background, or a radio headline that someone set fire to an O'Tolley's.<br /> <br /> Is it to show the beginning of unimaginable cosmic horror just beyond the veil of Kindred awareness? Then you're going to need to slow burn that like Dracula did with its &quot;the count is weird and I don't want to panic you fine Victorian folk, but he may, in fact, be a vampire&quot; material. Start slow with some corporate executives that even other corporate executives don't want to be around. They're beyond grimy, they go above and beyond the normal repulsiveness of people with ridiculously large sums of money for which they have no need. When your vampire characters go to dig up dirt on them (because they will, they're probably dealing with them in some capacity and that entails a lot of ground floor stuff for Kindred), have them encounter some uncommon resistance, possibly with individuals that the Kindred could mistake for freakish ghouls, because what could they be other than some Tzimisce experiment?<br /> <br /> After the Kindred prod a bit harder, have the secrets erupt like a geyser of pus and filth. The executives are engaged in Epstein shit, as a start, and go forward from there. At this point, the Kindred will also start to notice that they're being tailed by more &quot;ghouls&quot; that are getting harder and harder to write off as Tzimisce experiments, because there aren't nearly enough Tzimisce that are that deviant (or perhaps not normal enough?) to account for them, and digging into their personal lives reveals that they have nothing to do with vampires... but they might have crossed a Lupine. They might have scars that a Kindred recognizes as a calling card of being cut into by Lupine battle form claws (and somehow survived it; how the hell did they do that?). Maybe have a few confrontations with these &quot;freaks&quot; that go in directions that the Kindred aren't used to and don't like at all.<br /> <br /> And to shake them up extra hard, have the Lupines start paying more attention to the coterie. Have them drop uncharacteristically concerned warnings about the coterie's &quot;new friends and associates&quot;. Make the lower Humanity members, if any, subject to a bit more uncomfortable attention from both sides. Don't have banes try to possess them; banes generally know it's a bad move for any of them that's not a very particular type, and you can use this &quot;waiting for Bane-dot&quot; to have some of the more unhinged freaks start discussing certain members of the coterie and how &quot;someone is waiting for them&quot; or &quot;our friends want their turn, eventually&quot;.<br /> <br /> At this point, you can have a serious fight with one of the freaks where the coterie manages to cack some off, though, again, I must stress that you need to go for the lower powered among the possessed for this. Have the coterie all set to bring the body to one of their superiors to prove that some very weird shit is going on, and then it just... stops being weird as they watch. Have it happen again, so they either confirm that this shit happens or that they're starting to question their sanity. If you want to have another weird non- or less-confrontational Lupine encounter in neutral territory, have them drop cryptic hints about &quot;the seeds of evil drying up without something to parasitize&quot;. Have them absolutely not elaborate on that because the coterie still has no real idea what to believe, and the Lupines know it. Besides, it's close enough to everything that they need to know if they're really the galaxy brain city rulers that they claim they are.<br /> <br /> Have some of the bad news executives move in on the coterie's territory, then. This is a nice role reversal from the norm in W:tA where the Garou are usually getting messed up like this. The coterie's blood dolls start getting pushes around and stalked by the freaks that the coterie has fought, and a few that are newer and scarier. Have their hunting grounds start getting taken over, and then their havens bordering on some establishments that don't just lower property values, the people that the coterie relies on to be normal either disappear around them or they get &quot;invited in&quot; and aren't the same afterward. Give the Kindred a chance to rebuff them, after a fashion, and then have a subsidiary show up and start spending money more obviously to undo the efforts of the Kindred.<br /> <br /> After that, you've probably slow burned it enough. Have them spy on the arm of Pentex and see Lupines clash with truly fearsome freaks, which they overhear the Lupines call &quot;fomori&quot; or &quot;mockeries&quot;. While I'm not normally one for railroading, emphasize that those battlefields are absolutely not places that Kindred want to be, as even characters that the coterie knows to be resilient and harder to kill get turned into rotten meat and gore on the ground. Have any Auspex users detect faint shades of sickening feelings swirl around the freaks that die before inadvertently dispersing, possibly as an aura of rot glowing around a body that's turning normal. And, perhaps most horrifyingly, have the Lupines visibly have trouble with some of the freaks. It's only at this point that you really deal with nasty shit like Gorehounds and Throwbacks, or Ferectoi.<br /> <br /> Have some of the vampires who haven't witnessed these spectacles talk about how the Pentex reps aren't so bad, that they haven't noticed anything weird; make sure they do this early on and keep at it. Then have them show up with strange inclusions to their auras, perhaps even looking like they learned Vicissitude. Except that, again, the local Tzimisce don't know who they are or what they were doing, but can assure the coterie that no, this isn't their flesh-crafting art. (These are Kindred who have actually succumbed to being possessed by psychomachiae. Blood banes are the other option.)<br /> <br /> Then you can start having banes just show up without their flesh-clothes. Don't jump straight to a nexus crawler or other heavy hitter; leave that in reserve for if the coterie decides that they want to know more about what the fuck is going on and meet with the Lupines. This, naturally, gets around, and also paints the coteries as a loose end that won't stop being nosy or shutting up. Care also needs to be exercised here, as banes can shred most vampires as easily as the werewolves can if they're warrior types, and can even pack a wallop even if they aren't.<br /> <br /> At the session where you plan to end things, have a nexus crawler or dream bane pop up. Let the chips fall where they may for casualties; a number of coterie members are likely to die in sanity-defying ways, as will the werewolves. When the coterie asks what the hell it is, the Lupines, of course, have answers... that prompt only more questions.<br /> <br /> An epilogue can have the surviving members of the coterie pass by the gentrified remains of their former territory, now replete with various heads of the Pentex hydra, and start hallucinating monstrous features on others like when you finish the first Darkest Dungeon run in Darkest Dungeon. Now, like the werewolves, they will begin to see things as they truly are. Can they handle it, or will they just hoist Final Death on themselves and hope that they're still beyond the clutches of whatever they glimpsed in the shadow of the shadows?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-04T16:12:30-08:00","id":1555076,"title":"","content":"Loved this idea.<br /> <br /> Even if done otherwise, one important point for any real crossover with Pentex is that it's going to run on matters of ultimate evil.<br /> <br /> It doesn't matter how bad vampires usually are, the group will sooner or later have to take sides, because this is just too much. While Pentex isn't exactly a direct Wyrm's servant, it is now working for corruption and destruction in a scale and intensity most vampires never dreamed, not even the Sabbat. In fact, it's fairly easy (and in fact correct) for some Sabbat to conflate whatever Pentex is mingling with with true, actual demons.<br /> <br /> Vampires that deal with Pentex and choose to go with it are the worse scum possible, and will probably need a Path like <b>Evil Revelations</b>, unless they're still ignorant and doing lesser work. Even those are unknowingly risking their souls, though, in a sense most vampires thought already impossible, but turns out it isn't. Vampires that fight Pentex are going to have to really become better people, first because they're going to muster a lot of courage and selflessness just to try it, second because their own vileness will be a juicy weakness for the Wyrm to prey upon. This doesn't mean they have to become <b>good</b> people, just as not all Garou are good people, but certainly better than your usual vampire. Those who chose to not involve themselves are still going to have a rough ride, and possibly an even shorter life as they become a liability to everyone else.<br /> <br /> You may or may not have some Garou recognizing those vampires as allies by the end, but they'll nonetheless be forever changed by their brush with the notion of a ultimate evil lurking in the shadows of the world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}